Shared teaching-page infrastructure

Browser-native tools
--------------------
statistics.js supplies dependency-free probability functions and seeded draws.
browser.js and browser.css supply the shared Canvas and responsive interface.
Gibbs retains its own drawing helpers and styles.
lesson.js supplies the native lessons with shared controls, accessible tabs,
input errors, reproducible realizations, mobile settings and cached playback.
Animation reveals an existing simulation; it never redraws random observations.
statistics.js also supplies R-type-7 sample quantiles and histograms that retain
the probability mass of observations outside the displayed bin range.

html-tools.json is the authoritative catalogue for the published HTML tools,
their gallery order, lecture references, and explicit runtime assets.
All registered tools are native HTML. deploy.js builds a clean _site/ with no R,
Shiny, WebR or editor payloads.
GitHub Actions publishes only that artifact, not the historical docs/ exports.

Build and verify from the repository root (Node 22+):
  node deploy.js
  node TeachingTools/test_tools.js

browser_test.js runs Chrome through CDP using only Node built-ins. Set
HTML_SITE_ROOT=_site to test the published artifact instead of source pages:
  HTML_SITE_ROOT=_site node TeachingTools/test_tools.js --browser

test_tools.js discovers each catalogue entry's test_*.js numerical regressions
(excluding test_browser.js) and its test_browser.js interaction suite. The
lesson_browser_test.js helper adds shared regression checks for the lesson shell;
each tool adds its own statistical/control checks. Individual suites can still
be run directly with Node.

R/Shiny reference lessons
------------------------
All original R implementations are retained as references, but are not published
by the HTML workflow. apps.R reads the same html-tools.json catalogue. Its shared
flag refers only to the sixteen R references that use teaching.R, not the native
HTML runtime; new HTML-only lessons need no R reference. JavaScript and R use
different random generators, so equal seeds do not imply identical simulated
datasets.

teaching.R supplies their visual shell, reproducible random-number scope,
preset handling, and paused-by-default animation controls. Local apps source
../TeachingTools/teaching.R. Run them with shiny::runApp("SelectionEffects"),
replacing the directory as needed; shiny and bslib are required.

Reference tests:
  Rscript TeachingTools/test_numerics.R
  Rscript TeachingTools/test_pages.R

The numerical suite checks probability normalisation, exact updates, limiting
cases, simulation invariants, sampling distributions, and preservation of data
when inference or display settings change. The reactive suite exercises every
R teaching page, primary control boundaries, guided examples, plots, and animation.

deploy.R and test_export.R now delegate to the Node HTML build and export
tests; they no longer export or validate Shinylive payloads.
